#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079423} :activity/id #uuid "ff014b49-75da-5266-b4b5-9e7bd3e5b3d8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048186 :topic/id #uuid "869fb66c-d668-58cf-9dd4-b10651dbe9d5" :topic/math-academy-id 634} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079423 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079675 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the graph of $y=f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/2c/2cb61ee785ff6e04c5b73281389d6653f42bc93636da6840fbc2391ffb82451c.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$, how can we use graph transformations to plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(2x-4\\right)+1 \\,\n$$\n\nIn this particular situation, we have to combine several operations.\n\nTo perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must apply the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nIn this case, we begin by factoring out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny = f\\left(2x-4\\right) +1 = f\\left(2(x-2)\\right) + 1\n$$\n\nNow, to plot\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(2(x-2)\\right) +1\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(2x)$.\n\n![](images/07/07fe84bddbdc721dfba2fc9b720ca79ff794e9846fa1ef3bbc0c3c2217edd4e8.png)\n\n- Then, translate it to the right by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2(x-2)\\right)$.\n\n![](images/a1/a16ccd8e56c64f80f93476f2b7a41aa3093a2e4b9fdbe089fa502d1f1df15923.png)\n\n- There is no vertical scaling, so we omit this step.\n- Finally, translate it up by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2(x-2)\\right)+1$.\n\n![](images/f4/f4a894860fadbc3ac88fa360cae23ef0f33903f3d930cb00496bb2c2505d2334.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "bf539881-a530-50df-b39d-b9718839371f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4616 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e6e20122-6866-5fbc-9527-7a17b8c290e8" :step/math-academy-id 28409 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079425}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079424 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078510 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078360 :question/id #uuid "bbd2751e-73b2-500b-abef-c67564a9559f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7622"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c2387a4-202d-528a-b0ca-3f87d5a06ea3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079220}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Correct Order of Transformations Given a Transformed Function Expression"} :step/id #uuid "a78dc881-a3e2-5e40-bd1b-cf28fe897545" :step/math-academy-id 28411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078511 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078361 :question/id #uuid "c7dbe419-31b2-5c5a-bcab-738aa6fd46fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-7623"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17339421-a1ad-554f-aed8-28e20cebab10" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050085}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079218}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Transformations Resulting From Three Operations"} :step/id #uuid "a6368e4f-8808-5bd9-b6a8-0a2f253f3384" :step/math-academy-id 28412 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079426}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079426 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078512 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078362 :question/id #uuid "6ee9bee5-8027-5ba5-ad2c-9b697f2affc0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7624"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d69a3ef2-0854-503b-8586-b0113966d9ad" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079221}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Transformations Resulting From Four Operations"} :step/id #uuid "6856acd9-d60c-536e-80e4-da84599cde69" :step/math-academy-id 28413 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079424}}] :activity/title "Combining Graph Transformations: Three or More Operations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078658}]}
